<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><meta name="description" content="How Glassmacs discloses AI involvement in public documentation."/><title>{title}</title><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg"/><style>:root{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;color-scheme:light dark}body{margin:0;background:#f6f8fc;color:#182b42;line-height:1.75}header,main,footer{max-width:800px;margin:auto;padding:20px 26px}header{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:18px}a{color:#25628c}h1{font-size:clamp(2.5rem,6vw,4rem);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.05em}h2{margin-top:2em;line-height:1.2}h3{margin-bottom:0}p{font-size:1.06rem}li{margin:8px 0}footer{border-top:1px solid #dce5ef;margin-top:60px;font-size:.9rem}@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{background:#101b2a;color:#ecf3fb}a{color:#8ec9f0}footer{border-color:#31455c}}</style></head><body><header><a href="/">← Glassmacs</a><nav><a href="/story/">Our story</a> · <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a></nav></header><main><h1>Documentation Provenance</h1><p>Glassmacs uses AI extensively in software development, testing, and documentation. That makes it useful to describe not only what a page says, but how it was produced.</p><p><strong>Provenance is not a quality score.</strong> AI-generated prose can be correct or wrong. Human-written prose can also be correct or wrong. Provenance describes origin and transformation; evidence, review, tests, and citations address correctness.</p><h2>Why disclose this?</h2><p>Glassmacs is partly an experiment in whether AI-assisted development can make a small, maintainable macOS integration around upstream GNU Emacs possible—and make historical regression testing systematic. Its documentation should be equally transparent about AI's role. Readers should know whether a page was primarily AI-drafted, human-written, or produced through repeated collaboration.</p><h2>Human review and authorship are different questions</h2><p>A heavily AI-drafted page can be human-directed and reviewed. A mostly human-written page can depend on AI analysis. We distinguish three questions:</p><ol><li><strong>Generation:</strong> who or what produced the initial prose?</li><li><strong>Editing:</strong> who materially revised or reorganized it?</li><li><strong>Authority:</strong> who reviewed and approved the substantive direction or decision?</li></ol><p>A provenance label describes the first two. It does not, by itself, settle the third or guarantee accuracy.</p><h2>Reusable provenance labels</h2>{labels.map(([label,meaning])=><section><h3>{label}</h3><p>{meaning}</p></section>)}<h2>What the labels do not mean</h2><p>They do not imply that AI-generated material is unreviewed, that human-generated material is more accurate, that iterative work divides authorship equally by word count, that cited sources were AI-generated, or that provenance replaces tests and evidence. A label on a legal or policy document does not imply professional legal review.</p><h2>Version history and corrections</h2><p>Glassmacs's source and website live in a public repository. Commits, pull requests, issues, and CI results provide a more detailed history than a footer label alone. Public-facing claims should be corrected when contradicted by evidence. If legal or policy pages are added, they should use explicit effective and updated dates and preserve superseded public versions.</p><p>Read <a href="/story/">why the project exists</a>, consult the <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a>, or inspect the <a href="https://github.com/jmping/Glassmacs">public source and development history</a>.</p></main><footer>Documentation provenance: Iterative human–AI construction.</footer></body></html>